/lesson6.md", line_start:206, line_end:206, byte_start:11539, byte_end:11582, decoder:utf8, sha256:"e65e326da4a9d7c25cd512aeadf2ea1f42c80376db4e34ae2c06a4d6a6f3bc86"}, segment{id:"seg_8305b727c7be9ce979c1", path:"curriculum/im_teacher_guides/grade4/unit1/lesson6.md", line_start:208, line_end:208, byte_start:11675, byte_end:11702, decoder:utf8, sha256:"352d6758ba41459b8ce8d77693e357f0c7fe139b87cf11c287edf62d01ced2da"}, segment{id:"seg_609b76d9f35eb76b6bef", path:"curriculum/im_teacher_guides/grade4/unit1/lesson6.md", line_start:157, line_end:157, byte_start:7850, byte_end:7861, decoder:utf8, sha256:"4c104b5e7d1bc5818759db62ddc8f9ec6961615492d8e48e10075be6fbcf6ac3"}], source_evidence:task_evidence(rule(absent), source('curriculum/im_teacher_guides/grade4/unit1/lesson6.md', lines(157, 208)), position(student_task_statement(1)), excerpt("The picture shows lockers in a school hallway. The 20 students in Tyler\u2019s fourth-grade class play a game in a hallway that has 20 lockers in a row. The lockers are numbered from 1 to 20. \u2022 The 1st student starts with the 1st locker, and while going down the hallway, opens all the lockers. \u2022 The 2nd student starts with the 2nd locker, and while going down the hallway, shuts every other locker. \u2022 The 3rd student stops at every 3rd locker and opens the locker if it is closed or shuts the locker if it is open. \u2022 The 4th student stops at every 4th locker and opens the locker if it is closed or shuts the locker if it is open. \u2022 This process continues through the 20th student, so that all 20 students in the class touch the lockers. Create a representation to show what you understand about this problem. Consider: \u2022 How does your representation show lockers? \u2022 How does your representation keep track of students who touch lockers? \u2022 How does your representation show which lockers are open or closed?"), admission(unclaimed_student_task_statement))}).

